Core Position & Movement:
latitude & longitude->
What it is: The geographical decimal coordinates of the vehicle.
Why it's there: The foundational data points required by mapping libraries (Mapbox, Leaflet, Google Maps) to draw and update the bus marker on the screen.
timestamp->
What it is: The exact UTC date and time when the GPS hardware recorded the coordinate.
Why it's there: Mobile network packets arrive out of order or experience latency. The timestamp allows the frontend to discard stale pings, smoothly interpolate (animate) movement between updates, and detect when a tracker has gone offline.
speed_kmh->
What it is: The instantaneous speed of the vehicle in kilometers per hour.
Why it's there: Differentiates between a bus halted at a red light versus one stuck in bumper-to-bumper campus traffic. It is also used to calculate dynamic arrival estimates and suppress map jitter when the bus is completely stopped.
bearing->
What it is: The direction of forward travel in compass degrees (0° to 359°, with 0° being true North).
Why it's there: Orientates the bus icon along the road. Without bearing, the bus icon either points in an arbitrary fixed direction or slides sideways along turns.
accuracy_m->
What it is: The horizontal confidence radius of the GPS fix in meters.
Why it's there: Campus buildings and tree canopies cause multipath GPS signal bounce. If a tracker reports an accuracy radius of 45 meters, the backend can drop that ping rather than letting the bus visually "teleport" through a campus library or across a quad.
Routing & Identification:
bus_id->
What it is: The permanent identifier for the physical vehicle (e.g., registration number, chassis ID, or tracker IMEI).
Why it's there: Acts as the primary state key. The frontend uses bus_id to know which existing map marker to reposition instead of creating a duplicate icon for every ping.
route_id->
What it is: The designated line or loop the bus is serving (e.g., "BLUE-EXPRESS", "PERIMETER-LOOP").
Why it's there: Dictates the color-coding of the vehicle icon, associates the vehicle with a polyline drawn on the map, and allows users to filter the map view to only show the routes they care about.
trip_id->
What it is: The specific scheduled instance of a run along a route (e.g., "TRIP-ROUTE-A-0830").
Why it's there: Allows the system to compare real-time progress against a scheduled timetable to determine if a bus is running on time, early, or delayed.
Operational & Rider Experience:
is_in_service (or status)->
What it is: A flag indicating whether the vehicle is actively in rotation or unavailable.
Why it's there: Prevents "ghost buses." If a driver parks at the campus depot for lunch or maintenance, the GPS hardware keeps sending pings. Without this flag, students will wait at a stop for a bus that will not pick them up.
next_stop_id->
What it is: The unique identifier of the next scheduled stop the bus is approaching.
Why it's there: Powers UI cards and tooltips (e.g., "Next Stop: Student Union") directly. Providing this in the event avoids forcing client devices to run expensive spatial geofencing algorithms across hundreds of campus stops.
